1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,126
In a covalent bond, two non-metal atoms share a pair of electrons, forming a molecule.

2
00:00:05,126 --> 00:00:09,074
Two hydrogen atoms share their electrons so both get a full shell.

3
00:00:09,074 --> 00:00:13,846
Dot-and-cross diagrams show one atom's electrons as dots, the other's as crosses.

4
00:00:14,905 --> 00:00:18,146
An oxygen atom has 6 outer electrons and needs 2 more.

5
00:00:18,146 --> 00:00:24,038
It shares one pair with each of two hydrogen atoms, making a water molecule with two covalent bonds.

6
00:00:25,097 --> 00:00:31,519
Hydrogen chloride has one covalent bond. Methane has four, because carbon has four outer electrons to share.

7
00:00:31,519 --> 00:00:34,583
That's why carbon forms so many different molecules.

8
00:00:35,642 --> 00:00:40,709
Many substances we meet every day are made of small covalent molecules. Here are four.

9
00:00:41,768 --> 00:00:47,660
Every water molecule has an oxygen atom sharing a pair of electrons with each of two hydrogen atoms.

10
00:00:48,719 --> 00:00:55,023
The bubbles in fizzy drinks are carbon dioxide: a carbon atom joined to two oxygen atoms by covalent bonds.

11
00:00:56,082 --> 00:01:02,386
Natural gas for cooking is mostly methane. Carbon shares its four outer electrons with four hydrogen atoms.

12
00:01:03,445 --> 00:01:09,278
The oxygen we breathe is made of O₂ molecules, where two oxygen atoms share two pairs of electrons.

13
00:01:10,337 --> 00:01:14,344
These small molecules are all gases or liquids at room temperature,

14
00:01:14,344 --> 00:01:20,118
because the forces between the molecules are weak. Pause and think of one more covalent substance.
